    "acquired_at": "2026-11-11T09:07:57.990734+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-11-11T09:07:57.990734+00:00",
    "body": "To grow into a biopharma juggernaut, China aims to become the leading source of innovative medicines on the global markets—a goal that experts say the country is on track to hit.\nChina’s latest five-year plan to grow its domestic biopharma sector includes a set of goals that are largely within reach, experts told BioSpace —especially the country’s ambition to become a leading innovative engine for global biopharma, not merely a provider of cheap copycat drugs.\n“I have no doubt China will meet its goal,” Claudia Lin, founder of Biotech East West Technical Services, told BioSpace in an email.\nThat said, some of the targets laid out in the country’s most recent five-year plan, released by the Ministry of Industry and Information Technology in September, will be easier to attain than others.\nLin believes, for instance, that the Asian giant is all but guaranteed to have five blockbusters by the end of the decade and is on track to clear its 2030 revenue target of 3.5 trillion yuan ($521.8 billion). On the other hand, having 50 domestic drugmakers with 10 billion yuan ($1.5 billion) in annual revenue could be a tough target, said Cody Powers, principal at the management consulting firm ZS.\nMost of China’s heaviest hitters have been generating sales within the country, he explained, but “reaching this new milestone likely means many more companies expanding abroad.”\nChina\nChina plans to transform biotech sector into $522B powerhouse by 2030\nA new five-year biotech plan has been released by the Chinese government, revealing ambitious goals such as achieving five blockbuster medicines on the global markets in that time.\nSeptember 18, 2026\n·\n2 min read\n·\nAnnalee Armstrong\nRead more\nA long time coming\nChina’s rise as a go-to destination for biotech innovation and early-stage development has been strategic. The Asian country has long been releasing five-year plans for its biotech industry—the first came out in 2007—plotting priorities and specific goals to promote and guide the growth of its domestic drug development sector.\nChina has been “executing superbly” on its biopharma roadmap, Jeremy Levin, co-founder and executive chair of Ovid Therapeutics, said during a BioSpace webinar on China last month. “They’re faster, they’re better, they are doing what they said they’d do.”\nChina’s share of global licensing deal value this year has shot up to 58% , from 14% in 2023, according to an October report from global investment bank Locust Walk, as biopharma earmarks billions of dollars in partnerships with Chinese companies. “Any concept that they can’t do innovation is nonsense,” Levin said.\nThis despite increasing geopolitical pressures, including actions in the U.S. like the BIOSECURE Act that seek to restrict American companies from working with “foreign adversary biotech companies,” such as those in China.\nChina\nChina’s surging biotech sector represents both competition and lifeline for the US\nAs Chinese companies push beyond “me-too” drugs and the country lays out a new five-year plan targeting a $522 billion–strong biotech industry by 2030, experts say more predictable funding and regulation could help the U.S. maintain its edge.\nSeptember 23, 2026\n·\n8 min read\n·\nShawna Williams\nRead more\nMore recently, the U.S. Department of Treasury has proposed limiting outbound investments to biotech entities in certain countries of concern—a list that includes China. This measure, however, carves out a key exemption for licensing deals.\nSuch policy initiatives are therefore unlikely to stall China’s continued growth—or stop it from hitting its next set of goals, the foundation for which has already been laid.\n“China has set itself a target that most likely it would achieve without any further action on its part,” Mark Lansdell, director at life sciences analytics firm Evaluate, told BioSpace in an email. “The achievement of China’s end-of-decade goal was determined by actions that took place in the first years of the decade.”\nChina\nChina’s licensing deals soar as US biotechs enjoy a bumper M&A year\nChina may be taking the majority of licensing deals this year but those transactions don’t tell the whole story, according to Locust Walk, which points to the spike in M&A among U.S. biotechs as leveling the playing field.\nOctober 21, 2026\n·\n3 min read\n·\nAnnalee Armstrong\nRead more\nNot without challenges, need for changes\nThat said, the road will by no means be frictionless—and some of the country’s goals will prove more challenging than others. Achieving 50 homegrown companies with $1.5 billion in revenue will be “the hardest to hit,” according to ZS’ Powers.\n“There’s already a decent-sized list of companies at that level,” Powers told BioSpace over email. This includes Hengrui Pharmaceuticals ($4.74 billion revenue in 2025), Fosun Pharma ($6.25 billion) and Hansoh Pharmaceutical ($2.25 billion).\nTo cross the 50 mark, China will likely need to change its approach to dealmaking, Powers said. Currently, the country’s dominant model has been to license out worldwide rights to domestically developed drugs, collecting only local revenue once drugs hit the market. To top $1.5 billion, Powers argued, Chinese biotechs will need to retain ex-China ownership of the drugs they make. “If companies just out-license, it will be pretty hard to reach that goal.”\nAside from a shift in partnership strategy, there are also some broader, cultural changes that need to happen for China to truly grow into a global biopharma powerhouse, Biotech East West Technical Services’ Lin said. “China has always won world-class status by its speed, cost and volume,” she said. Now, the country “needs to shift the culture surrounding the industry and [make] quality a focus.”\nLin recognizes that China has made major improvements in quality and compliance over the years, particularly after joining the International Council for Harmonization—a global group that sets quality standards for producing pharmaceuticals—in 2017.\nStill, novel therapeutic modalities are proving challenging for the country’s ascendant biotech sector, Lin said, pointing to the need to ensure that these cutting-edge technologies are safe and effective, and that data generated from their studies are reliable.\nOf course, a more aggressive push to expand ownership overseas is likely to be met by pushback from other countries, Powers recognizes. Meeting this 50-company goal, therefore, also depends on “whether U.S. and European policymakers restrict that growth through trade policy,” he said. “[Future] policy obstacles as the trade war expands into biopharma could hinder launches or otherwise act as a headwind.”\nAnd given that vulnerability, China would do well to “improve domestic economics,” in addition to expanding into overseas markets, Powers argued. He noted that the country’s healthcare authorities have continued to negotiate lower prices, cutting into biopharma companies’ domestic revenue.\nFive blockbusters only ‘a matter of time’\nOf all of China’s 2030 goals, having five blockbusters appears most achievable. Meeting the target “is largely now a matter of time,” Evaluate’s Lansdell said.\nFor a drug to have a good chance of making $1 billion by 2030, it needs to already be on the market or in late-stage development, which means China laid the groundwork for this goal years ago. “The wheels have already been set in motion,” Lansdell explained.\nEvaluate’s internal data projects at least six blockbusters for China by the end of the decade—two of which have already crossed that bar and just need to maintain their market performance. These include BeOne Medicines’ BTK inhibitor Brukinsa, which made $3.9 billion last year , and Legend Biotech’s Johnson & Johnson-partnered CAR T therapy Carvykti, with $1.9 billion in 2025 sales .\nBehind these, the products likely to cross the blockbuster line by 2030 are BeOne Medicines’ PD-1 inhibitor Tevimbra —approved in the U.S. for esophageal and gastric cancers and forecast to pass $1 billion in annual sales as early as next year—and Summit Therapeutics’ Akeso-partnered ivonescimab, a PD-1xVEGF bispecific antibody that Evaluate estimates will hit $4.4 billion in sales between the partners by 2030.\nChina\n6 potential blockbusters that could pave China’s path to 2030 ambitions\nIn its latest five-year plan, China has set its sights on developing five drugs that could each rake in more than $1 billion globally by 2030. Experts say it’s well on its way, with two medications having already met that bar.\nNovember 4, 2026\n·\n8 min read\n·\nTristan Manalac\nRead more",
